    <comment>New package: soapy-audio - the SoapySDR backend for soundcard-class receivers.

Why it belongs in Factory: Factory already ships soapy-sdr and seven of its
hardware plugins (soapy-airspy, soapy-bladerf, soapy-hackrf, soapy-osmo,
soapy-remote, soapy-rtlsdr, soapy-uhd). soapy-audio is the direct peer for the
one remaining common hardware class - receivers that present themselves as a
sound card (Softrock and similar quadrature front-ends on a line-in, SDR
transceivers exposed as audio devices) - with optional hamlib rig control for
tuning. Without it the set of SoapySDR backends in Factory is incomplete, and
every SoapySDR frontend in Factory silently lacks that device class.

It is submitted from hardware:sdr, which is already the registered devel project
for soapy-sdr and all seven plugins above, so it lands where its peers are
maintained and follows their spec layout (same soapy_modver / module naming).

License: MIT (upstream LICENSE.txt). Two notes recorded as a Legal-Review-Notice
in the spec so they need not be re-derived on each scan:
 - cmake/Modules/FindRtAudio.cmake is BSD-3-Clause (Idiap); LibFindMacros.cmake
   and Findhamlib.cmake carry no notice at all. All three are build-system
   helpers and are never compiled into a shipped artifact.
 - the bundled RtAudio is MIT (its extra &quot;please send modifications upstream&quot;
   sentence is explicitly non-binding); its notice ships as LICENSE.RtAudio.txt.

Source: upstream last tagged 0.1.1 in 2019 and publishes no releases, so this
ships a pinned master snapshot (0.1.1+git20251009.63b22ec, obs_scm). Master is
the 0.1.1 tree plus exactly the two hamlib patches this package used to carry
downstream, plus a cmake_minimum_required bump and an exception-by-const-ref
fix. Pinning master therefore lets the package ship patch-free and stop relying
on the distro-wide CMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM shim. It is not an FTBFS fix -
the 0.1.1 tag still builds today via that shim. '+git' is deliberate: the '~git'
form would sort below the 0.1.1 tag.

Bundled RtAudio, declared as Provides: bundled(rtaudio) = 5.1.0. The module
builds upstream's in-tree RtAudio 5.1.0 rather than linking Factory's rtaudio 6:
RtAudio 6 removed the RtAudioError API that SoapyAudio uses and changed device
enumeration from a 0..count-1 index to opaque ids from getDeviceIds(), which
SoapyAudio does not implement. A compile-only fix (as Debian carries) leaves
device enumeration wrong at runtime, so the correct port belongs upstream rather
than in a first Factory submission.

No proprietary content in the bundled copy: Factory's own rtaudio.spec strips
include/ and tests/Windows (the Steinberg ASIO SDK headers) in %prep. The
in-tree copy here is only README.md, RtAudio.cpp and RtAudio.h, so there is
nothing equivalent to remove.

Debian is the only other distro carrying this package (soapyaudio 0.1.1-7,
forky/sid; not in Fedora or Arch). Their 01-rtaudio-6.patch changes Streaming.cpp
only - exceptions to error codes - and leaves Registration.cpp's index-based
device enumeration untouched against rtaudio 6's opaque ids, which is why
keeping the in-tree copy is the correct call here rather than the lazy one.

Backend: PulseAudio only, which also covers PipeWire. RtAudio selects the first
compiled-in API whose getDeviceCount() is non-zero and RtApiPulse unconditionally
reports one device, so any second backend compiled in beside it would be
unreachable dead code rather than a fallback.

Built natively on aarch64 against Factory, and hardware:sdr built the submitted
revision green on Tumbleweed x86_64 and aarch64 (plus Leap 16.0). rpmlint reports
0 errors, 0 warnings, 0 badness on both arches; %check verifies that SoapySDR
actually loads the built module.

ADDED IN THIS REVISION
Explicit &quot;Requires: soapy-sdr&quot; on the module subpackage, at dimstar's request
on SR 1374694 (soapy-plutosdr), applied to all five plugin submissions: &quot;Shouldn't that require soapy-sdr? The module
on its own seems quite useless&quot;.

To be precise about what this does and does not change: the dlopen itself lives
in libSoapySDR (SoapySDR::loadModules(), reached from Device::make()), which the
module already pulled in through its automatic soname dependency on
libSoapySDR.so.0.8-3 - so any application linking the library could always load
the module. What nothing pulled in was the soapy-sdr package, which ships
SoapySDRUtil; the module could therefore be installed on a system with no
SoapySDR client at all to enumerate or probe it. So this is a usability
dependency rather than a functional one, and the spec comment says so. The
soname dependency remains the ABI-exact constraint, which is why the new line is
deliberately unversioned instead of duplicating the ABI in the spec.

Stated plainly so it is not mistaken for convention: none of Factory's existing
SoapySDR plugins - soapy-airspy, soapy-bladerf, soapy-hackrf, soapy-osmo,
soapy-remote, soapy-rtlsdr, soapy-uhd - carries such a Requires. This change
makes these five stricter than current Factory practice; it is what the reviewer
asked for, not a convention being matched.

Rebuilt natively on aarch64 after the change: green, rpmlint unchanged, and
&quot;rpm -qp --requires&quot; on the built module now lists both
libSoapySDR.so.0.8-3()(64bit) and soapy-sdr.

Supersedes SR 1374701.</comment>
